Third Session
Five Policy Questions

Question: Since antiquity, when emperors and kings have governed All under Heaven, none have failed to attach supreme importance to moral human relationships. Such was the case when Yao brought harmony to the nine branches of his kin, when Shun beautified the Five Canons, when Cheng Tang initiated the cultivation of human moral bonds, and when King Wu attached great weight to the people's five teachings. Reflecting upon our august dynasty: Emperor Gao, the Grand Progenitor (Taizu), with the virtue of a supreme sage, ruled as sovereign over the myriad multitudes. In his leisure from the ten thousand affairs of state, he compiled the Three Collections of Grand Pronouncements (Dagao sanbian) item by item to instruct his officials and subjects; entries therein such as "Educating the People in the Five Teachings" (Wujiao yumin) and "Reaffirming the Five Constant Virtues" (Shenming wuchang) all earnestly fostered moral human relationships. Emperor Wen, the Grand Ancestor (Taizong), composed the Unseen Rewards of Doing Good (Weishan yinzhi) and the True Records of Filial Piety (Xiaoshun shishi), and Emperor Zhang, the Proclaiming Ancestor (Xuanzong), authored the Book of the Five Human Relationships (Wulun zhi shu), promulgating them throughout the realm; neither failed to attach supreme importance to moral human relationships. The Three Sages were of a single mind, and their writings tally like matching tallies and follow the same tracks as the texts of the Canons, Counsels, Instructions, and Announcements (Dian, Mo, Xun, Gao), to be transmitted alongside them for ten thousand generations into the distant future. Yet as for how their words and…
